CAT = "aatl2JournalTb"; SEG = "JNL"
Q = [
("Which transaction is recorded first in the journal rather than in a daybook?",
 "Writing off an irrecoverable debt", ["A credit sale to a customer", "Goods sent back by a credit customer", "An invoice from a supplier"],
 "The journal records transactions that no other book of prime entry covers, such as irrecoverable debt write-offs, opening entries, payroll and error corrections."),
("A new business starts with £12,000 in the bank, a van worth £14,500 and a bank loan of £6,500. The opening journal credits capital with:",
 "£20,000", ["£26,500", "£33,000", "£18,500"],
 "Capital = assets - liabilities = (£12,000 + £14,500) - £6,500 = £20,000. £26,500 is the assets alone."),
("A customer owing £540 including VAT at 20% is written off as irrecoverable, and the VAT can be reclaimed. The journal debits irrecoverable debts with:",
 "£450", ["£540", "£90", "£432"],
 "Net = £540 / 1.2 = £450, debited to irrecoverable debts, with £90 debited to VAT. Taking 20% off the gross gives £432."),
("When an irrecoverable debt is written off, which account is credited?",
 "Receivables ledger control", ["Irrecoverable debts expense", "Sales", "VAT control"],
 "The customer no longer owes the money, so the receivables ledger control account (and the customer's own account) is credited. The expense is the debit."),
("Gross pay is £42,600. Employees' income tax is £5,980, employees' NIC £2,710 and employees' pension contributions £2,130. Net pay is:",
 "£31,780", ["£33,910", "£36,620", "£29,650"],
 "£42,600 - £5,980 - £2,710 - £2,130 = £31,780. Forgetting the pension deduction gives £33,910."),
("Employer's National Insurance contributions are:",
 "An extra cost to the employer", ["Deducted from employees' gross pay", "Income of the business", "Never shown as an expense"],
 "Employer's NIC is paid on top of gross pay, so it is part of the wages expense. Employees' NIC is the part deducted from their pay."),
("The total cost of employing staff (gross pay plus employer's NIC and pension) is journalled as:",
 "Debit wages expense, credit wages control", ["Debit wages control, credit wages expense", "Debit HMRC, credit wages expense", "Debit bank, credit wages control"],
 "The full cost goes to the wages expense, with the wages control account as the other side. Wages control is then cleared by paying net pay, HMRC and the pension scheme."),
("Income tax and NIC deducted under PAYE are credited to:",
 "An HMRC liability account", ["The wages expense account", "The bank account", "The capital account"],
 "PAYE deductions and NIC are owed to HMRC until paid, so they are credited to an HMRC liability account. Paying HMRC later debits that account."),
("Gross pay is £18,000, employer's NIC £1,950 and employer's pension contributions £540. The wages expense for the period is:",
 "£20,490", ["£18,000", "£19,950", "£18,540"],
 "Wages expense = gross pay + employer's NIC + employer's pension = £18,000 + £1,950 + £540 = £20,490."),
("The cost of a new delivery van is debited to the vehicle running expenses account. This is an:",
 "Error of principle", ["Error of commission", "Error of original entry", "Error of omission"],
 "An asset has been posted to an expense, a different class of account. That is an error of principle. An error of commission uses the wrong account of the right class."),
("A credit sale to P Kerr is posted to the account of P Kerry in the receivables ledger. This is an:",
 "Error of commission", ["Error of principle", "Error of omission", "Error of original entry"],
 "The right type of account was used, but the wrong one. The debit and credit are still equal, so the trial balance does not show it."),
("An invoice for £1,290 is entered in the daybook as £1,920, and both sides are posted using £1,920. This is an:",
 "Error of original entry", ["Error of commission", "Compensating error", "Error of principle"],
 "The wrong figure was used at the start, so both debit and credit are wrong by the same amount and the trial balance still agrees."),
("Money received from a customer was debited to the receivables ledger control account and credited to bank. This is a:",
 "Reversal of entries", ["Error of principle", "Error of commission", "Error of omission"],
 "The right accounts and amount were used, but debit and credit were swapped. Correcting it takes twice the amount on each side."),
("Which of these errors does the trial balance reveal?",
 "A debit posted with no credit", ["A transaction left out entirely", "An entry to the wrong customer", "A capital item posted as an expense"],
 "A one-sided entry makes debits and credits differ. Omissions, commissions and principle errors keep both sides equal, so the trial balance still balances."),
("A trial balance totals £148,320 debit and £147,870 credit. A suspense account is opened with:",
 "£450 credit", ["£450 debit", "£900 credit", "£225 credit"],
 "The credit side is £450 short, so the suspense account takes a £450 credit balance to make the trial balance agree."),
("Rent of £680 was credited to bank correctly but debited to the rent account as £860. The journal to correct it is:",
 "Debit suspense £180, credit rent £180", ["Debit rent £180, credit suspense £180", "Debit suspense £860, credit rent £860", "Debit rent £680, credit bank £680"],
 "Rent is overstated by £860 - £680 = £180, so rent is credited £180. The difference had gone to suspense as a credit, which is cleared by the £180 debit."),
("Discounts allowed of £120 were debited to the discounts allowed account with no other entry. The journal to correct it is:",
 "Dr suspense, Cr receivables control", ["Dr receivables control, Cr suspense", "Dr discounts allowed, Cr suspense", "Dr suspense, Cr discounts allowed"],
 "The missing credit to receivables ledger control is made now, and the matching debit clears the £120 credit in suspense."),
("An office expense of £350 was left out of the books completely. Does it create a suspense account entry?",
 "No, both sides are missing", ["Yes, a £350 debit", "Yes, a £350 credit", "Yes, a £700 credit"],
 "An error of omission leaves out the debit and the credit, so the trial balance still agrees and no suspense entry arises."),
("One error overstates a debit by £200 and another overstates a credit by £200. Together they are:",
 "Compensating errors", ["Errors of principle", "Reversal of entries", "Errors of omission"],
 "The two errors cancel each other out in the totals, so the trial balance agrees even though both accounts are wrong."),
("In an initial trial balance, the capital account balance of £42,000 goes in the:",
 "Credit column", ["Debit column", "Either column", "Notes only"],
 "Capital is what the business owes its owner, so it has a credit balance. Assets, expenses and drawings are debits."),
("Which balance appears in the debit column of a trial balance?",
 "Drawings", ["Capital", "Sales", "Bank loan"],
 "Drawings reduce what the business owes the owner, so they are a debit. Capital, sales and loans are credit balances."),
("Motor expenses have a debit balance of £4,260. A journal moves £1,100 of it to motor vehicles at cost. The adjusted motor expenses balance is:",
 "£3,160 debit", ["£5,360 debit", "£3,160 credit", "£1,100 debit"],
 "The journal credits motor expenses £1,100: £4,260 - £1,100 = £3,160, still a debit. Adding it gives £5,360."),
("After all errors have been corrected, the suspense account should have:",
 "No balance left", ["A debit balance", "A credit balance", "The original difference"],
 "The suspense account only holds the difference while errors are found. Once each is journalled, it should be cleared to nil."),
("An initial trial balance does not balance and the difference cannot yet be found. What is the usual next step?",
 "Open a suspense account for it", ["Add the difference to the capital", "Ignore it if it is small", "Post it to sundry expenses"],
 "A suspense account holds the difference so work can continue while the errors are found and corrected by journal."),
]
